1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,300
Welcome to Kieu Studio, The Art of Coding.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:10,333
Here, LeetCode is only the practice material.
What we really train is problem-solving.

3
00:00:10,333 --> 00:00:18,600
For every problem we repeat four steps: understand
the problem, form a hypothesis, test it, and reflect.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:27,733
Today, episode 3 is problem #44, Wildcard Matching. It
is a Hard problem, but we will take it step by step.

5
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:36,433
We are given a string s and a pattern p. We must
decide whether the pattern matches the entire string.

6
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:40,967
In the pattern, a question mark
(?) matches exactly one character.

7
00:00:40,967 --> 00:00:45,800
A star (*) matches any sequence of
characters, including an empty one.

8
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:53,500
A lowercase letter matches only itself. And the match
must cover the whole string, not just part of it.

9
00:00:54,533 --> 00:01:00,733
The input is a string s and a pattern
p. The output is true or false.

10
00:01:00,733 --> 00:01:06,667
Both s and p have length from 0 to
2000, so either one can be empty.

11
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:15,033
The string s has only lowercase English letters.
The pattern p has lowercase letters, ? and *.

12
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:23,600
2000 × 2000 = 4 million. Remember this number:
it tells us which approaches are still usable.

13
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:31,333
According to the LeetCode page on 28
October 2026, this problem is rated Hard.

14
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:40,400
There are 967,654 accepted submissions, out
of about 2.9 million submissions in total.

15
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:48,167
The acceptance rate is 33.1%. These are
submissions, not the number of people who solved it.

16
00:01:48,167 --> 00:01:54,533
A Hard problem is not an impossible problem.
It just needs a good thinking process.

17
00:01:55,567 --> 00:02:03,333
Example 1: the string s is
"aa" and the pattern p is "a".

18
00:02:03,333 --> 00:02:07,467
The a in the pattern matches
the first a in the string.

19
00:02:07,467 --> 00:02:16,700
But the string still has one a left, while the pattern has
ended. A partial match is not enough, so the answer is false.

20
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:22,067
Lesson 1: always check the
ending condition on both sides.

21
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:30,500
Example 2: the string s is still "aa",
but the pattern p is a single star.

22
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:38,800
A star stands for any sequence of characters. It
swallows the first a, then swallows the second a too.

23
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:43,667
The string and the pattern end
together, so the answer is true.

24
00:02:43,667 --> 00:02:53,433
Lesson 2: one symbol can create many choices. A
star can swallow zero, one, two characters, or more.

25
00:02:54,433 --> 00:03:02,633
Example 3: the string s is
"cb" and the pattern p is "?a".

26
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:08,233
The ? matches exactly one
character, here the letter c.

27
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:16,033
Next the pattern needs an a, but the string has
a b. The letters differ, so the answer is false.

28
00:03:16,033 --> 00:03:24,233
Lesson 3: without a star, everything is forced into
line, and one position off ruins the whole match.

29
00:03:25,267 --> 00:03:34,867
Three examples, three habits: check the ending condition, find where
choices appear, and notice when everything is forced into line.

30
00:03:34,867 --> 00:03:42,000
We have not written a single line of code, but we
already know the hard part of the problem is the star.

31
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:49,033
Before seeing the solution, try thinking
on your own. Here are three hints.

32
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:57,867
Hint 1: the only place with many choices is the star.
Ask yourself how many characters the star should swallow.

33
00:03:57,867 --> 00:04:05,933
Hint 2: shrink the problem. Do the first i characters of
the string match the first j characters of the pattern?

34
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:16,133
Hint 3: once you have something that works, ask: on a mismatch, do
we need to go back to every star we passed, or just some of them?

35
00:04:16,133 --> 00:04:22,067
Think for the next ten seconds: when you meet
a star, which choice would you try first?

36
00:04:32,067 --> 00:04:38,900
Ten seconds are up. Whether or not you have an
answer, let us start from the simplest idea.

37
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:49,133
Put a pointer i on the string s and a pointer j on the pattern p.
The small question is: from positions i and j, does the rest match?

38
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:57,200
If p[j] is a letter or a ?, compare one character,
then move both pointers forward one step.

39
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:04,833
If p[j] is a *, we have two choices. One is to
skip the star, which means it swallows nothing.

40
00:05:04,833 --> 00:05:11,333
The other is to let the star swallow one more
character, and keep the star so it can swallow again.

41
00:05:11,333 --> 00:05:18,933
When the pattern ends, the string must end too. That
is exactly the ending condition from our first lesson.

42
00:05:19,967 --> 00:05:33,667
Let us run that idea on a small example: s is "aaa" and p
is "*a*b". The answer is false, so we must try every branch.

43
00:05:33,667 --> 00:05:43,767
Each node is a pair of positions (i, j). The left branch
skips the star. The right branch swallows one more character.

44
00:05:43,767 --> 00:05:49,533
When a branch fails, we go back and
try another one. That is backtracking.

45
00:05:49,533 --> 00:05:59,033
Look closely: some pairs (i, j) appear more than once, highlighted
in yellow. We are solving the same question again and again.

46
00:05:59,033 --> 00:06:05,267
The tree makes 20 function calls, but
there are only 14 different questions.

47
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:11,267
The number of calls grows very quickly
as the pattern gets more stars.

48
00:06:11,267 --> 00:06:22,033
We ran it for real. With a string of 20 a's, a pattern
with one star needs 62 calls; six stars need 203,078.

49
00:06:22,033 --> 00:06:29,433
With lengths up to 2000, trying every
branch is impossible. We need a better way.

50
00:06:30,467 --> 00:06:37,467
Try a faster idea: when we meet a star,
let it swallow nothing, and never go back.

51
00:06:37,467 --> 00:06:51,133
Run it on s = "adceb" and p = "*a*b". The first star swallows
nothing, a matches a, and the second star also swallows nothing.

52
00:06:51,133 --> 00:06:58,967
Now the b in the pattern meets the d in the
string: a mismatch. This idea concludes false.

53
00:06:58,967 --> 00:07:06,033
But the correct answer is true, because the second
star can swallow d, c and e, and then b matches.

54
00:07:06,033 --> 00:07:16,633
The lesson: a good hypothesis can still be wrong. We need a counterexample
to find out, and the star must get a chance to swallow more.

55
00:07:17,633 --> 00:07:24,033
The safe approach: never solve an old
question again. Store the results in a table.

56
00:07:24,033 --> 00:07:31,667
Cell dp[i][j] answers: do the first i characters of
the string match the first j characters of the pattern?

57
00:07:31,667 --> 00:07:39,800
An empty string matches an empty pattern. And an empty
string only matches a pattern made entirely of stars.

58
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:49,000
If the pattern character is a letter or a ?, the cell is true when
the characters match and the cell diagonally above-left is true.

59
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:58,367
If it is a *, the cell is true when the cell above is true (swallow
one more) or the cell to the left is true (swallow nothing).

60
00:07:58,367 --> 00:08:06,033
Fill the table for s = "adceb"
and p = "*a*b", one row at a time.

61
00:08:06,033 --> 00:08:10,167
The last cell is true, so the answer is true.

62
00:08:10,167 --> 00:08:22,467
Within the problem's limits, the table has over 4 million
cells, each computed once: time O(m × n), memory O(m × n) too.

63
00:08:23,467 --> 00:08:32,367
Look at the table again: each cell only needs the row above
and the cell beside it. We are remembering more than we need.

64
00:08:32,367 --> 00:08:41,500
Think about the star. It can swallow anything. So once a
segment after a star has matched, everything before it is fine.

65
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:48,633
A segment between two stars should match as early as
possible, leaving the most characters for what comes next.

66
00:08:48,633 --> 00:08:57,100
So on a mismatch, we only need to go back to the most
recent star. Older stars never need to be retried.

67
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:07,567
Four variables: i on the string, j on the pattern, star for
the latest star, and mark for how far that star has swallowed.

68
00:09:07,567 --> 00:09:13,433
If the characters match or the pattern
has a ?, move i and j together.

69
00:09:13,433 --> 00:09:21,533
If the pattern has a *: remember star and mark, then
move only j, letting the star swallow nothing first.

70
00:09:21,533 --> 00:09:32,300
On a mismatch with a star available: let it swallow one more character.
Increase mark, set i to mark, and set j right after the star.

71
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:37,633
On a mismatch with no star yet: nothing
can save it, the answer is false.

72
00:09:37,633 --> 00:09:46,900
When the string ends, skip any remaining stars at the end of
the pattern. The answer is true only if the pattern is used up.

73
00:09:47,933 --> 00:09:56,967
Start: j meets the first star. Remember star
= 0, let it swallow nothing, and move j.

74
00:09:56,967 --> 00:10:02,867
The a in the pattern matches the a
in the string. Both i and j move.

75
00:10:02,867 --> 00:10:10,533
The second star: remember star = 2 and
mark = 1, and again let it swallow nothing.

76
00:10:10,533 --> 00:10:20,733
The b in the pattern meets d: a mismatch. The
second star swallows d, and j goes back to b.

77
00:10:20,733 --> 00:10:29,367
Another mismatch with c, so the star swallows c.
Then a mismatch with e, so the star swallows e.

78
00:10:29,367 --> 00:10:38,500
This time b matches b. The string has ended
and the pattern is used up: the answer is true.

79
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:43,633
Before writing Python,
describe the logic in words.

80
00:10:43,633 --> 00:10:54,067
Loop while the string has characters: match, move on; star,
remember it; mismatch, go back to the star; no star, return false.

81
00:10:54,067 --> 00:11:02,167
After the loop, skip any extra stars at the end of the pattern,
then check whether j has reached the end of the pattern.

82
00:11:03,167 --> 00:11:07,867
Here is the complete Python 3
solution, only about 20 lines.

83
00:11:07,867 --> 00:11:15,567
Setup: i and j are 0, star is -1
meaning no star yet, and mark is 0.

84
00:11:15,567 --> 00:11:19,900
The main loop runs while i is less
than the length of the string.

85
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:28,567
Branch one: if there is pattern left and the
characters match, or it is a ?, increase i and j.

86
00:11:28,567 --> 00:11:35,200
Branch two: on a star, save
star and mark, then increase j.

87
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:44,800
Branch three: with a star available, increase mark and
reset i and j. Last branch: with no star, return false.

88
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:53,300
After the loop, skip the stars at the end of the pattern.
The answer is true when j has reached the end of the pattern.

89
00:11:54,333 --> 00:12:05,833
Let us watch the code run with s = "adceb" and p =
"*a*b". On the right are the variable values at each line.

90
00:12:05,833 --> 00:12:11,000
The line star = j: the
first star is remembered.

91
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,733
The a matches: i and j both increase.

92
00:12:14,733 --> 00:12:19,400
The second star is remembered: star = 2.

93
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:29,700
b mismatches d, so the program takes branch
three: mark increases, i = mark, j = star + 1.

94
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:36,100
Two more mismatches, with c and e:
the star swallows two more letters.

95
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:41,333
Finally b matches b. i = 5, and the loop ends.

96
00:12:41,333 --> 00:12:49,700
The last line: j = 4, exactly the length
of the pattern. The function returns true.

97
00:12:50,700 --> 00:12:57,833
We do not rely on reasoning alone. The three
official examples give exactly the expected results.

98
00:12:57,833 --> 00:13:09,400
We compared it with two independent methods, a
regex and a DP table, on 173,355 exhaustive cases.

99
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:14,200
Plus 20,000 random cases.
Not a single mismatch.

100
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:21,300
In the worst case, a string of 2000
a's, the main loop runs 1,002,001 times.

101
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:26,533
Experiments are strong evidence, but
they are not a mathematical proof.

102
00:13:27,567 --> 00:13:30,233
Let us compare the three approaches.

103
00:13:30,233 --> 00:13:39,900
Trying every branch: calls grow very fast with the number of
stars. Measured: over 203,000 calls for just 20 characters.

104
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:52,133
The DP table: time O(m × n), memory O(m × n). Within the
problem's limits that is over 4 million cells, which still runs.

105
00:13:52,133 --> 00:13:59,300
Two pointers: memory O(1). The
worst case is still O(m × n).

106
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:07,133
On random data, we measured an average of 0.592 loop
iterations per character of the string and pattern.

107
00:14:07,133 --> 00:14:14,833
No approach is always best. Choose based on the
limits, the memory, and how easy it is to prove.

108
00:14:15,867 --> 00:14:21,133
One: read the requirement carefully,
especially the ending condition.

109
00:14:21,133 --> 00:14:26,500
Two: find where the choices come
from. In this problem, it is the star.

110
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:32,567
Three: start with something simple and
correct, then measure why it is slow.

111
00:14:32,567 --> 00:14:37,767
Four: if the same question
repeats, remember the result.

112
00:14:37,767 --> 00:14:44,867
Five: find structure that lets you remember
less, like keeping only the most recent star.

113
00:14:44,867 --> 00:14:51,367
Six: always verify with examples,
counterexamples, and an independent method.

114
00:14:51,367 --> 00:14:58,167
These habits work beyond LeetCode: when
you plan, fix bugs, or design a system.

115
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:03,067
Three problems to keep
practising, from easy to hard.

116
00:15:03,067 --> 00:15:11,367
Problem #392, Is Subsequence, Easy: two pointers
on two strings, a warm-up for greedy thinking.

117
00:15:11,367 --> 00:15:19,433
Problem #72, Edit Distance, Medium: define the table
cell yourself, and the three choices at each cell.

118
00:15:19,433 --> 00:15:29,600
Problem #10, Regular Expression Matching, Hard: there the star
means something different. Compare it with today's problem.

119
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:39,200
Do not look at code right away. Ask yourself: what is the small
question, where are the choices, and do you need to remember results?

120
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:43,633
Remember how to think, not just the code.

121
00:15:43,633 --> 00:15:52,900
Thank you for watching. See you in the next episode of
Kieu Studio, The Art of Coding. More resources at kieu.app.
